{
  "image_name": "0f8cfa96da8f20d885c30a4a66722b65.png",
  "prompt_text": "This figure summarizes how kallikrein-related peptidases (KLKs) released by cancer cells reshape the tumor microenvironment. The top part shows tumor cells secreting KLKs, which then act outside the cells on junctional and adhesion molecules. Visually, KLKs are depicted cleaving proteins such as desmoglein/desmocollin, E-cadherin, and interacting with integrin-linked contacts, leading to weaker cell–cell junctions, altered cell–matrix attachment, and remodeling of the surrounding extracellular matrix (ECM). Overall, the message is that KLKs help cancer cells disengage from neighboring cells and modify their local matrix.\n\nThe lower part expands this into broader ECM remodeling consequences. KLKs appear to activate or regulate other protease systems, including uPA, plasmin, MMPs, and meprins, and to affect major ECM components such as fibronectin, collagens, laminins, CCN proteins, and hyaluronan. These changes generate bioactive ECM fragments, alter matrix composition, activate latent pro-factors like HGF, TGFβ, and EGF, and release growth factors including EGF, FGF, PDGF, and VEGF. The overall implication is a protease-driven network that influences blood vessels, stromal and immune cells, cancer cells, and metastatic niches.",
